WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:07.800
The identity operator, is, is used to see if two&nbsp;
variables are taking up the same memory space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:13.760
The identity operator is important when speed is&nbsp;
of utmost importance, as it is often in a game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:21.760
situation. To demonstrate the identity operator,&nbsp;
I have this 145-identity file open. The code is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.480
similar to the code in the 135-identity file.&nbsp;
The code contains colors and ranks for teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:42.840
used in a game. The same value is assigned to&nbsp;
teams 1 and 3 on lines 1 and 3. Team 2 is set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:51.280
to be equal to Team 1, as the code indicates on&nbsp;
line 2. The two print statements on lines 5 and 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:57.440
use the is keyword to see if team1 and team2 use&nbsp;
the same memory space, which saves memory space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:07.120
and can make an app run more efficiently, and if&nbsp;
team1 and team3 use the same memory space. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:20.080
run this code, and we get one True result and one&nbsp;
False result. Team1 is Team2 because team2 did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:29.320
have a specific value assigned to it. Instead, it&nbsp;
took the value of team1, meaning it is a variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:37.000
that uses the value in team1’s memory space.&nbsp;
Conversely, team1 is not team3 because, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.280
though the value of team3 is the same as that of&nbsp;
team1, the value was explicitly assigned to team3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:52.400
giving team3 its own memory space. To further show&nbsp;
the difference between identity and comparison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:01.200
operators, I will add a print statement to line 7&nbsp;
showing whether team1 equals team3. I will rerun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:12.440
the code and value-wise, team1 and team3 have the&nbsp;
same values so that print statement returns true.

